9	// The QSA selection over **block** scores instead of cell scores (P14-1), and
10	// the dispatch that deletes `llm_attn_expand.comp` altogether.
11	//
12	// `llm_attn_select.comp` is the same radix select over a tensor
13	// `llm_attn_expand.comp` writes: one f32 a cache cell a token, `nKV` of them,
14	// which at 128 000 cells and a 2048-row batch is **1.14 GB of arena** and five
15	// streams of it per token — four radix passes and the emit. But the tensor has
16	// only `nKV/ratio + 1` distinct values in it, because **all `ratio` cells of a
17	// pooled block carry one block score** (llm_attn_score.comp) and every cell
18	// the causal mask drops carries -inf. So the select can run over the block
19	// scores with a per-block **weight** — how many of its cells this token can
20	// see — and get the same threshold over a quarter of the traffic, with the
21	// expansion not computed at all.
22	//
23	// `ratio` is 4 in this model, so that is 4x on every pass and the arena goes
24	// with it. P13's budget at 128 000 cells put `attn.select` at 0.233 ms a token
25	// and `attn.expand` at 0.051 — 16% of a prefill token there — and the freed
26	// arena is what a wider ubatch at depth needs, which is the other half of the
27	// same change.
28	//
29	// **It is the same answer, bit for bit, and that is what makes it allowed.**
30	// Three things have to hold and all three are arithmetic rather than hope:
31	//
32	//  1. **The threshold.** A radix select needs the count of keys above a
33	//     prefix; a weighted histogram over blocks counts exactly the cells the
34	//     per-cell histogram counted, because every cell of block b has key b and
35	//     nothing else does. `desired` starts at `min(selWidth, ncols)` as before
36	//     and the total weight is exactly `ncols`.
37	//  2. **The tie fill.** The reference appends cells equal to the threshold in
38	//     ascending cell index (L4a, and the note in llm_attn_select.comp), and the
39	//     emit below still walks cells ascending — it is the *keys* that come from
40	//     blocks, not the order. Two blocks whose scores happen to be equal are
41	//     one bucket in the histogram and two runs in the emit, and the emit takes
42	//     the lower run first, which is what the per-cell code did.
43	//  3. **The dead cells.** Everything past this token's position is -inf, whose
44	//     key `0x007FFFFF` is the smallest there is, and they are all *after* the
45	//     live cells — so they enter the histogram as one weighted entry and the
46	//     emit fills them from the lowest index, again as before. A block can
47	//     never collide with that key: the only bias that makes one -inf is the
48	//     `b >= nBid` arm, and those blocks have weight zero.
